      "math_academy_id": "q-184797",
      "problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a,b\\in Z,$ if $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20),$ then $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na={{field-6}},\\,b={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThen, $5a=$ {{field-8}} and $5b=$ {{field-9}}. \n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{20}_{⏟}}_{5a}{{field-10}}{{40}_{⏟}}_{5b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{4}_{⏟}}_{a}{{field-11}}{{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nSo, the pair $a=$ {{field-12}} and $b=$ {{field-13}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
